g  the labor force  changes the way that employers do  business so for example if you have a  labor force that is highly educated and  highly productive you basically have to  structure your business models  differently than if you have a huge  number of workers who don't have a lot  of skills right so for example if you  have a country that has a very highly  educated population it's a lot more  likely that you'll use machines to do a  lot of back-breaking physical work then  that you'll use huge armies of people  you know if you have to pay high wages  that's another reason to economize on  labor right when you think about  American history one of the reasons that  this is the country that created  interchangeable parts you know that  innovated in all sorts of ways in all  sorts of labor saving ways it's partly  because on the frontier for example you  know we didn't always have a huge  abundance of labor it led to the use of  ingenuity and creativity so when it  comes to employers and their agenda I  would argue that different employers  have pretty different agendas if you're  an employer who thinks that hey my labor  force is mostly in China you know what I  do here is I have some research and  design and you know kind of so you're  not really worried as much about getting  access to cheap labor right maybe you're  thinking well I just want to be sure  that I can get the best engineers for  example and the best engineers they can  be pretty expensive but you're not as  concerned about that there are other  businesses however where their business  model would cease to exist without  low-cost labor so in that case they  might be really motivated to secure as  much low-cost labor as they can but I  don't think that business is monolithic  on this front I think that you actually  see really big divides among different  kind of businesses depending on the  business models they depend on but happy  to discuss that that a greater length  still I'd like to go back to the  question you raised about sponsorship  I'm agent enough to realize that my  father after World War two signed it  signed into the country any number of  uncles aunts cousins with the proviso  that they would not be unwell fair for  two years  and a number of them slept on the  living-room couch for about six months  until they could get their feet on the  ground when did that change this is a  wonderful question and it is it really  speaks to a kind of revolution in the  welfare state so if you're looking at  folks who secure green cards in the  United States right now unless you  entered the less you secured your status  as a refugee or as an acai Li in those  cases you're immediately eligible for a  wide swath of safety net benefits if you  aren't in one of those categories you  are subject to a temporary five-year ban  it's called on accessing a range of  different safety net benefits but that  ban has a lot of holes running through  it you know for one thing if you have  kids they're not subject to that band  right it's subject to the actual you  know kind of initiating person the adult  and then there's the fact that there are  all sorts of things that are not  technically safety net benefits that  really function as safety net benefits  for example the Earned Income Tax Credit  the Earned Income Tax Credit is a  provision of the tax code which says  that if your wages fall below a certain  level you will get a refund check  quote-unquote from Uncle Sam that is  designed to defray payroll taxes or more  than that but you know that is something  that really makes a big difference for a  lot of folks and it's you could argue a  kind of welfare right so in that case  you know you are immediately eligible  for that and it's kind of a funny thing  because people who say that we have a  desperate need for lots of low-skilled  low-wage labor what they don't  acknowledge is that well if we had such  a desperate need for it clearly the  wages would be somewhat higher right we  wouldn't need the Earned Income Tax  Credit and other a fundable tax credits  to really top-up people's incomes so I  think that you know that is a really  tricky thing you know there are some  people who say that oh these immigrants  they're lazy they don't know I mean  they're immigrants who do work the  problem is that in many cases their  skills are such that they can't really  command high enough incomes to support  themselves and their families now the  Trump administration  proposed revising the public charged  status basically revising how we think  of what makes you a public charge or not  right now the rules we have on the books  our rules that were established by Bill  Clinton in 1999 and under those rules  you're really only considered a public  charge if you get all of your income  from cash welfare or if you're literally  being institutionalized but if you  collect you know if you rely on Medicaid  if you you know collect snap if you  collect the EITC it's all of these other  things you wouldn't count as a public  charge President Trump has said let's  try to fix that let's try to have a more  modern and someone argue sensible  conception of what it means to be a  public charge which says that we have  this expectation that you are going to  support yourself now to the point about  families here's a hilarious story so  under federal law since 1996 if you are  sponsoring a relative you really are  responsible and if that relative does  collect a safety net benefit the public  sector has the right to engage in  sponsor recovery to collect money from  the person who sponsored that immigrant  guess what sponsor recovery has never  ever happened Mike Bloomberg the former  mayor of New York tried to do it he  asked for a voluntary contribution from  folks who sponsored immigrants and you  know he collected in a city of eight and  a half million people he collected about  five hundred thousand bucks when bill de  blasio came back into office he said  this is an outrage and he returned the  money so basically this thing that is  literally on the books is never enforced  and whenever anyone talks about  enforcing this very basic common-sense  provision they're run out of town on a  rail it's a the idea is that this is  some kind of terrible awful thing now  there was one proposal Republicans in  the house proposed in the good luck bill  what they said is you know right now if  you were the parent of a US citizen you  can enter the country without limit it's  an unlimited number of folks who can  come now you know again you know if  you're the parent of a US citizen you  might be an elderly person you might be  an elderly person who needs a nursing  home you might be someone who relies not  just on Medicare but also in Medicaid  and a variety of other benefits now you  know there are some kind of limits on  that here and there  but you know fundamentally we ought to  do in my opinion with the Canadians do  what the Canadians say is that you can  bring your adult parent that's great  you're a wonderful person  congratulations for being such a dutiful  child but it's going to be on a  temporary renewable five year visa and  you only keep the visa if you purchase  health insurance for that parent now to  me that seems like a pretty sensible  idea we're not saying you cannot bring  the relative what we're saying is that  you must be held responsible if you're  gonna be so wonderfully generous well be  wonderfully generous right and I do  think that bringing that principle back  into our immigration system that you  describe would make a world of  difference and by the way I just want to  stress there are some sponsors who take  their responsibilities seriously but  there are a whole heck of a lot of  others who aren't even educated about  what their responsibility is and what it  means law serves a tutelary function  when we do not enforce some of these  laws we send people a very bad message  that these laws are to be ignored  they're not to be taken seriously that  this supposed responsibility is really  just a piece of paper and that also has  lots of negative consequences for how we  think about assimilation thanks for  coming on late-night TV there's often a  lot of skits that are comedic of the man  on the street that doesn't understand  civics history or social studies  whatever you want to talk about it you  used the word assimilation so many times  tonight but you haven't mentioned  specifically how the people that are  here illegally that you want to keep  here can assimilate and understand the  trouble that those people behind you  went through to craft the Constitution  to understand the Constitution and to  abide by the Constitution what are your  specific ideas well sure sure sure there  are many different pieces to your  question one is this question  civic education for all Americans  whether they're foreign border  native-born I've got to tell you I think  that civic education for native-born  folks is not where it ought to be in my  humble opinion and I do think this is  something we need to invest in and take  seriously when it comes to unauthorized  migrants you know I've got to say I  think that this population is a  population that is aging very rapidly  you are talking about a population where  the share that is over the age of 50 is  growing enormous Lee and you know by the  time you're looking at you know 10 or 15  years from now you're gonna look at  folks who are not really the seedbed of  the next generation these are not people  who are going to be shaping our civic  life unfortunately or fortunately you  know kind of whatever your view on this  is these are folks who oftentimes have  spent you know most of their adult lives  in the United States they've done it in  very low wage work they've done it  really toiling in the shadows they've  done it without any kind of big idea of  taking part in our civic life if you  look at that older amnesty from 1986 we  talked about at the beginning of the Q&amp;A  session one of the really striking  things is that of the roughly 3 million  folks who are legalized actually huge  numbers of them never became naturalized  citizens you actually had a wave of them  who became naturalized citizens in 96  why is that because in 96 you started  talking about well one hypothesis is  that you started talking about limiting  safety net benefits for folks who were  in citizens so then there was like an  immediate visceral reason to kind of get  involved in a political fray but there's  a huge number of people they just wanted  the legal status they weren't thinking  you know I want to vote in elections or  something like that they just you know  wanted to be assured that they're not  gonna get kicked out of the country  that's not necessarily a good thing but  when we're thinking about the practical  on-the-ground realities of that you know  that's one thing to keep in mind and  when you're thinking about folks who  were granted some kind of legal status  you know one way to do that is to say  that okay if you came into the country  as a minor then okay maybe you might  eventually become a citizen but if you  did not come into the country as a minor  you know there are a lot of folks who  might say well I don't think it's  entirely fair to say that that person  ought to jump ahead of many other people  who enter the country lawfully  to become a citizen right that doesn't  mean that civic education doesn't matter  but it does present a different set of  questions and challenges I'd be happy to  talk to you about that further but look  you know I agree with you a hundred  percent civic education matters a huge  amount it absolutely matters for  immigrants but I do believe that this is  a problem that's not a niche issue that  just applies to this one group of folks  it applies to all Americans it is the  duty of all of us to teach our children  about our constitutional ideals to make  sure that they continue to be defended  and cherished for future generations  thank you very much  [Applause]
 

